Hello everyone.

I would like to ask you one question, I think a Yes or Not is enough, but long responses are wellcome too.

I'm working in a Questionnaire with 34 questions, all of them in Likert Scale response (1-5) (NOT CONTINUOUS). I've adjusted a model with 5 factors. I used for it "Factor" a software developed by Lorenzo-Seva (2013) et al. focus in categorical variables.

The results have sense but I would like to do a CONFIRMATORY ANALYSIS.

Is lavaan capable to do this kind of Analysis?

The model would be:

model <- '
   # latent variables
     labor =~ v1 + v3 + v5 + v6 + v8 + v10
     delivery =~ v2 + v4 + v7 + v9 + v11
     SupportBaby =~ v12 + v13 + v14 + v15 + v16
     midwiwes =~ v17 + v19 + v21 + v23 + v25 + v27 + v29 + v31 + v33
     doctors =~ v18 + v20 + v22 + v24 + v26 + v28 + v30 + v32 + v34
'

Thank you for your time.

From u99a06696c483481a at groups.google.com  Mon Jul  7 16:32:12 2014
From: u99a06696c483481a at groups.google.com (Edward Rigdon)
Date: Mon, 07 Jul 2014 16:32:12 +0000
Subject: Re: CFA doubt
Message-ID: <9B_j0BJMuM8.2YyMD0ijSlMJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <9B_j0BJMuM8.WifrG56mqPgJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <9B_j0BJMuM8.WifrG56mqPgJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/9B_j0BJMuM8/m/2YyMD0ijSlMJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

David--
     I think the primary issue is that statistical identification for your higher order factors is weak.  Each factor needs at least two dependents plus  a correlation with something else.  You are almost at the very minimum of this condition.  As a result, standard errors for the path estimates related to these higher order factors will be large, and confidence intervals will be wide.  It may be that the model is misspecified, but with parameter values just outside the acceptable range, I think that the weak identification is the more likely problem.
     If that is true, then you might address the problem by making the statistical identification more firm. That would mean either more dependents per factor, more correlates per factor, or (probably most effective) fewer free parameters.  If you have prior knowledge that would let you fix some of the parameter estimates to "known" values, then your other, free parameter estimates would become more stable, and you might obtain more acceptable estimates.
     You might also consider bootstrapping.  Perhaps if you re sample your data repeatedly, you will see that the mean values across replications are actually within the acceptable range.  That would be easy enough to set up and run.
--Ed Rigdon

Sent from my iPad

